  "statements": [
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-1",
      "quote": "The course has covered what AI *is* and what it *can do*; this chapter is the part that decides what it *can afford to do* — and it is missing until now.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-1",
      "section": "",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-2",
      "quote": "Economics is not a business afterthought bolted onto the technology; it is a **physical layer**, and it explains decisions you have already seen without a word of arithmetic: why the frontier moved to [Mixture of Experts](mixture_of_experts.php) (more knowledge, same active cost), why the field moved from large under-trained models toward smaller models trained on far more data ([Scaling](hyperparameters.php), \\cite[Hoffmann et al., 2022]{hoffmann2022chinchilla}), and why \"just run it locally\" is sometimes the cheapest option and sometimes the most expensive.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-1",
      "section": "",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-3",
      "quote": "The whole layer reduces to two numbers — *FLOPs for training* and *FLOPs per token for inference* — and everything below is arithmetic on top of those two.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-1",
      "section": "",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-4",
      "quote": "floating-point operations.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-4",
      "section": "The 6ND rule: training cost in one line",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-5",
      "quote": "The \"6\" has a clean origin, and it is worth deriving rather than memorizing:",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-4",
      "section": "The 6ND rule: training cost in one line",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-6",
      "quote": "Price your training run Pick a model size, a data budget, a GPU, and an MFU.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-5",
      "section": "The 6ND rule: training cost in one line",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-7",
      "quote": "Watch the FLOPs, hours, and dollars move.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-5",
      "section": "The 6ND rule: training cost in one line",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-8",
      "quote": "GPU A100 (~312 TFLOP/s BF16) H100 (~495 TFLOP/s TF32) H100 BF16 (~989 TFLOP/s)",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-6",
      "section": "The 6ND rule: training cost in one line",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-9",
      "quote": "Inference is a **forward pass only** — no backward — so the per-token cost equals one training step's forward pass, about **2 FLOPs per active parameter per token** (one third of the $6N$ used for a full training step):",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-9",
      "section": "Inference cost per token: active parameters, not total",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-10",
      "quote": "Price your tokens Enter a price per million tokens and a session's token count.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-10",
      "section": "Inference cost per token: active parameters, not total",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-11",
      "quote": "Slide the cached fraction to see the effect of prompt caching on the bill.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-10",
      "section": "Inference cost per token: active parameters, not total",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-12",
      "quote": "Tokens per session (millions) Cached fraction 60%",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-11",
      "section": "Inference cost per token: active parameters, not total",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-13",
      "quote": "The single most important serving cost-saver is **prompt caching**, and its arithmetic is the [prefill/decode split](inference_vs_training.php) you already met.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-14",
      "section": "Why caching saves ~10×: the prefill is paid once",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-14",
      "quote": "The expensive part of a call is often the **prefill** — reading a long, *repeated* system prompt, tool definitions, or few-shot examples into context.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-14",
      "section": "Why caching saves ~10×: the prefill is paid once",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-15",
      "quote": "Without caching, you pay the prefill FLOPs *every call*.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-14",
      "section": "Why caching saves ~10×: the prefill is paid once",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-16",
      "quote": "With caching, the repeated prefix's keys and values are computed **once** and *reused*, so the second and later calls skip that prefill almost entirely.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-14",
      "section": "Why caching saves ~10×: the prefill is paid once",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-17",
      "quote": "That is where the \"~10×\" comes from: when a call is dominated by a long, repeated prefix (say 4,000 tokens of system prompt) plus a short completion (say 400), uncached you pay for all 4,400 tokens' prefill+decode, but cached you pay mostly for the 400-token completion — roughly **one tenth** of the work per call.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-15",
      "section": "Why caching saves ~10×: the prefill is paid once",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-18",
      "quote": "\\cite[Anthropic, Prompt caching]{promptcaching} is the production example: cached tokens are billed at a fraction of uncached ones, precisely because the prefill that would have been recomputed is not.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-15",
      "section": "Why caching saves ~10×: the prefill is paid once",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-19",
      "quote": "The calculator above models this directly — slide the cached fraction and watch the bill fall by up to ~90% on the cached part.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-15",
      "section": "Why caching saves ~10×: the prefill is paid once",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-20",
      "quote": "The deeper point: caching is not a trick, it is a consequence of the two regimes.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-16",
      "section": "Why caching saves ~10×: the prefill is paid once",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-21",
      "quote": "**Prefill is compute-bound and pay-once-able; decode is memory-bound and per-step.** Anything you can make *pay-once* (a repeated prefix) you should cache, because you are moving cost out of the per-call path and into a one-time one.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-16",
      "section": "Why caching saves ~10×: the prefill is paid once",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-22",
      "quote": "The last comparison is the one people get wrong.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-18",
      "section": "7B on a laptop, or the API: fixed cost vs per-token cost",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-23",
      "quote": "\"Run it locally\" and \"call the API\" are not the same cost shape:",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-18",
      "section": "7B on a laptop, or the API: fixed cost vs per-token cost",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-24",
      "quote": "They cross at a **breakeven**: local is better when your *steady* token volume is high enough that the hardware cost is recovered within your horizon; the API is better when your volume is light, spiky, or private-data-light.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-19",
      "section": "7B on a laptop, or the API: fixed cost vs per-token cost",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-25",
      "quote": "And there is a third axis the arithmetic ignores — **privacy**: local keeps the data on the machine, which is sometimes worth the worse economics.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-19",
      "section": "7B on a laptop, or the API: fixed cost vs per-token cost",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-26",
      "quote": "This is the [Running locally](running_locally.php) and [Inference optimization](inference_optimization.php) decision, made into a number.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-19",
      "section": "7B on a laptop, or the API: fixed cost vs per-token cost",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-27",
      "quote": "> The economic layer is two numbers and one split.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-20",
      "section": "7B on a laptop, or the API: fixed cost vs per-token cost",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-28",
      "quote": "Training costs $6ND$; inference costs 2× active-params per token.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-20",
      "section": "7B on a laptop, or the API: fixed cost vs per-token cost",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-29",
      "quote": "Caching moves the pay-once prefill out of the per-call path; the active-vs-total gap is what sparsity buys; and fixed-versus-per-token is what \"local or API\" really means.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-20",
      "section": "7B on a laptop, or the API: fixed cost vs per-token cost",
      "kind": "statement"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-stmt-30",
      "quote": "None of it is about the technology being *worth* it — it is about the technology being *affordable*, which is a different question, and the one that decides what gets built.",
      "block_id": "ai_economics-block-20",
      "section": "7B on a laptop, or the API: fixed cost vs per-token cost",
      "kind": "statement"
    }
  ],
  "blocks": [
