    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-block-8",
      "quote": "Inference cost per token: active parameters, not total",
      "kind": "heading",
      "section": "Inference cost per token: active parameters, not total"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-block-9",
      "quote": "Inference is a **forward pass only** — no backward — so the per-token cost equals one training step's forward pass, about **2 FLOPs per active parameter per token** (one third of the $6N$ used for a full training step):",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "Inference cost per token: active parameters, not total"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-block-10",
      "quote": "Price your tokens Enter a price per million tokens and a session's token count. Slide the cached fraction to see the effect of prompt caching on the bill.",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "Inference cost per token: active parameters, not total"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-block-11",
      "quote": "Tokens per session (millions) Cached fraction 60%",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "Inference cost per token: active parameters, not total"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-block-12",
      "quote": "Paying for it in practice",
      "kind": "heading",
      "section": "Paying for it in practice"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-block-13",
      "quote": "Why caching saves ~10×: the prefill is paid once",
      "kind": "heading",
      "section": "Why caching saves ~10×: the prefill is paid once"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-block-14",
      "quote": "The single most important serving cost-saver is **prompt caching**, and its arithmetic is the [prefill/decode split](inference_vs_training.php) you already met. The expensive part of a call is often the **prefill** — reading a long, *repeated* system prompt, tool definitions, or few-shot examples into context. Without caching, you pay the prefill FLOPs *every call*. With caching, the repeated prefix's keys and values are computed **once** and *reused*, so the second and later calls skip that prefill almost entirely.",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "Why caching saves ~10×: the prefill is paid once"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-block-15",
      "quote": "That is where the \"~10×\" comes from: when a call is dominated by a long, repeated prefix (say 4,000 tokens of system prompt) plus a short completion (say 400), uncached you pay for all 4,400 tokens' prefill+decode, but cached you pay mostly for the 400-token completion — roughly **one tenth** of the work per call. \\cite[Anthropic, Prompt caching]{promptcaching} is the production example: cached tokens are billed at a fraction of uncached ones, precisely because the prefill that would have been recomputed is not. The calculator above models this directly — slide the cached fraction and watch the bill fall by up to ~90% on the cached part.",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "Why caching saves ~10×: the prefill is paid once"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-block-16",
      "quote": "The deeper point: caching is not a trick, it is a consequence of the two regimes. **Prefill is compute-bound and pay-once-able; decode is memory-bound and per-step.** Anything you can make *pay-once* (a repeated prefix) you should cache, because you are moving cost out of the per-call path and into a one-time one.",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "Why caching saves ~10×: the prefill is paid once"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-block-17",
      "quote": "7B on a laptop, or the API: fixed cost vs per-token cost",
      "kind": "heading",
      "section": "7B on a laptop, or the API: fixed cost vs per-token cost"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-block-18",
      "quote": "The last comparison is the one people get wrong. \"Run it locally\" and \"call the API\" are not the same cost shape:",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "7B on a laptop, or the API: fixed cost vs per-token cost"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-block-19",
      "quote": "They cross at a **breakeven**: local is better when your *steady* token volume is high enough that the hardware cost is recovered within your horizon; the API is better when your volume is light, spiky, or private-data-light. And there is a third axis the arithmetic ignores — **privacy**: local keeps the data on the machine, which is sometimes worth the worse economics. This is the [Running locally](running_locally.php) and [Inference optimization](inference_optimization.php) decision, made into a number.",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "7B on a laptop, or the API: fixed cost vs per-token cost"
    },
    {
      "id": "ai_economics-block-20",
      "quote": "> The economic layer is two numbers and one split. Training costs $6ND$; inference costs 2× active-params per token. Caching moves the pay-once prefill out of the per-call path; the active-vs-total gap is what sparsity buys; and fixed-versus-per-token is what \"local or API\" really means. None of it is about the technology being *worth* it — it is about the technology being *affordable*, which is a different question, and the one that decides what gets built.",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "section": "7B on a laptop, or the API: fixed cost vs per-token cost"
    }
  ]
}
